    "case_id": "173",
    "case_summary": "A 28-year-old G2P1 woman presented 31 days after an elective cesarean delivery under spinal anesthesia with worsening headache and easy fatigability for 3 days. The headache was continuous, global, and had become severe over 24 hours, associated with nausea and malaise. Eight days postoperatively she had developed a mild global headache managed with rest and paracetamol. By day 31 the headache was severe, nonpositional, and unrelieved by rest or analgesics. Vital signs were normal. She was fully conscious and oriented, all meningeal signs were negative, and motor function was intact. Laboratory studies showed a normal complete blood count, prothrombin time, partial prothrombin time, and international normalized ratio. Noncontrast CT of the head revealed a layered hypodense crescent-shaped extra-axial collection over the left frontoparietal convexity with areas of linear and crescentic hyperdensity, measuring 15 mm in width with a 13-mm midline shift.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Chronic subdural hematoma\nB. PDPH was considered because of recent dural puncture but was excluded since “In PDPH, the pain worsens or develops\nC. Meningitis was considered but was excluded as “All meningeal signs were negative\nD. Coagulopathy\nE. Intracerebral (intra\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
